---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../components/ProjectCard.astro';
import ArchiveRow from '../components/ArchiveRow.astro';

const projects = (await getCollection('projects'))
  .sort((a, b) => (a.data.order ?? 99) - (b.data.order ?? 99));

const talks = [
  {
    date: 'AUG 27 2026',
    title: 'claude code',
    venue: 'Claude community event',
    href: 'https://github.com/justanotherspy/talks/tree/main/claude-code/27-aug',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="justanotherspy" active="home">
  <div class="stack-3" style="margin-bottom: 56px;">
    <div class="label">/ A PERSONAL SITE /</div>
    <h1 class="display">small tools,<br />built to be <span class="amp">read</span>.</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width: 56ch; margin-top: 6px;">
      I&rsquo;m <span class="redact"><span>NameRedact</span></span>, a security and infrastructure engineer based in <span class="redact"><span>LocationRedact</span></span>.
      I build small command line tools, most of them for working with Claude Code, and each one gets a proper write-up here: what it does, what shaped it, and what it refuses to do.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="stack-4" style="margin-bottom: 64px;">
    <div class="row-between">
      <h2 class="section"><span class="sec-num">01</span>projects</h2>
      <a href="/projects" class="link" style="font-size:13px;">All projects →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="home-projects-grid">
      {projects.map(p => (
        <ProjectCard
          href={`/projects/${p.id}`}
          tag={p.data.tag}
          title={p.data.title}
          description={p.data.description}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="stack-4" style="margin-bottom: 64px;">
    <h2 class="section"><span class="sec-num">02</span>the claude code plugin ecosystem</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-prose">
        <p>
          garlic and shuck started as ordinary CLIs, but I spend most of my day inside <a class="link" href="https://code.claude.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code</a>, so each one also ships as a plugin.
          garlic&rsquo;s plugin runs the same time-tracking hooks and adds a <code class="inline">/garlic</code> command.
          shuck&rsquo;s runs a background monitor that feeds the session new CI failures and review comments as they happen, plus a <code class="inline">/shuck</code> skill for asking on demand.
        </p>
        <p>
          All of them are catalogued in one central marketplace, <a class="link" href="https://github.com/justanotherspy/claude-plugins" target="_blank" rel="noopener">justanotherspy/claude-plugins</a>.
          Each plugin lives in its own tool&rsquo;s repo and the marketplace just points at it, so a plugin is always in sync with the binary it drives.
          Add the marketplace once, then install whichever plugins you want.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-hdr"><span>CLAUDE CODE</span><span>PLUGINS</span></div>
        <pre class="code-body"><span class="tc"># add the marketplace once</span>
<span class="tk">&gt;</span> /plugin marketplace add justanotherspy/claude-plugins
&nbsp;
<span class="tc"># then pick what you want</span>
<span class="tk">&gt;</span> /plugin install garlic@justanotherspy
<span class="tk">&gt;</span> /plugin install shuck@justanotherspy</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="stack-4" style="margin-bottom: 64px;">
    <h2 class="section"><span class="sec-num">03</span>the homebrew tap</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-prose">
        <p>
          The binaries come from <a class="link" href="https://github.com/justanotherspy/homebrew-tap" target="_blank" rel="noopener">justanotherspy/homebrew-tap</a>.
          I never edit the casks by hand: every release pipeline regenerates its own cask and pushes it to the tap, and a release only flips to latest once its binaries and cask are both in place.
          The tap can&rsquo;t point at a half-published release.
        </p>
        <p>
          shuck and sproot install on macOS and Linux. garlic&rsquo;s cask is macOS only; on Linux it installs with <code class="inline">cargo install garlic-ward</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-hdr"><span>BASH</span><span>INSTALL</span></div>
        <pre class="code-body"><span class="tc"># tap once</span>
<span class="tk">$</span> brew tap justanotherspy/tap
&nbsp;
<span class="tc"># then install by short name</span>
<span class="tk">$</span> brew install --cask garlic
<span class="tk">$</span> brew install --cask shuck</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="stack-4">
    <div class="row-between">
      <h2 class="section"><span class="sec-num">04</span>talks</h2>
      <a href="https://github.com/justanotherspy/talks" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link" style="font-size:13px;">The talks repo →</a>
    </div>
    <p class="split-prose" style="max-width: 56ch;">
      I give the occasional talk at Claude community events, and the slides all land in <a class="link" href="https://github.com/justanotherspy/talks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">justanotherspy/talks</a>.
      If a talk leaves you with questions, open an issue there and I&rsquo;ll answer.
    </p>
    <div>
      {talks.map(t => (
        <ArchiveRow href={t.href} date={t.date} title={t.title} meta={`${t.venue} · slides ↗`} external />
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
